examples.solution_text:
Stand at $B$, facing north. The north lines are parallel.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.967,1.199);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (A)--(0,2) node[above]{N};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (B)--(2.967,3.199) node[above]{N};
\draw (A)--(B);
\fill (A) circle (1.5pt); \fill (B) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below left] at (A){$A$}; \node[right] at (B){$B$};
\draw (0,0.65) arc[start angle=90,end angle=22,radius=0.65];
\node at (0.7,1.05){$068^\circ$};
\draw[answerblue] (2.967,1.749) arc[start angle=90,end angle=202,radius=0.55];
\node[text=answerblue] at (2.15,1.75){$c$};
\draw[answerblue,-{Stealth}] (2.967,2.049) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-158,radius=0.85];
\node[text=answerblue] at (4.1,0.7){$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Co-interior angles on parallel lines add to $180^\circ$.
$\begin{aligned}c&=180^\circ-68^\circ\\&=112^\circ\end{aligned}$
Angles at a point add to $360^\circ$.
$\begin{aligned}b&=360^\circ-112^\circ\\&=248^\circ\end{aligned}$
The bearing of $A$ from $B$ is $248^\circ\text{T}$.

Draw north lines at $L$ and $S$, and mark the given bearing at $L$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (L) at (0,0); \coordinate (S) at (-3.118,0.72);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (L)--(0,2) node[above]{N};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (S)--(-3.118,2.72) node[above]{N};
\draw (L)--(S);
\fill (L) circle (1.5pt); \fill (S) circle (1.5pt);
\node[below] at (L){$L$}; \node[left] at (S){$S$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0.85) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-193,radius=0.85];
\node at (1.1,-0.65){$283^\circ$};
\draw[answerblue] (0,0.5) arc[start angle=90,end angle=167,radius=0.5];
\node[text=answerblue] at (-0.65,0.8){$c$};
\draw[answerblue,-{Stealth}] (-3.118,1.47) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-13,radius=0.75];
\node[text=answerblue] at (-2.1,1.45){$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
First find the remaining angle at $L$.
$\begin{aligned}c&=360^\circ-283^\circ\\&=77^\circ\end{aligned}$
Use co-interior angles on the parallel north lines.
$\begin{aligned}b&=180^\circ-77^\circ\\&=103^\circ\end{aligned}$
The bearing of $L$ from $S$ is $103^\circ\text{T}$.

